      "writtenOn": "2021-09-08T14:20:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I seem to remember adding it at the beginning as it wouldn\u0027t compile without it. I don\u0027t know if things have changed, or if I had done something wrong at the time, but it isn\u0027t needed now and it breaks a small but not negligible part of the Android Studio user base with older machines.\nMy understanding from looking at the code (SkCpu) is that Skia will automatically detect what instructions the CPU it is running on supports, and adapt automatically to take advantage of what features are available. So newer CPUs should still get some benefit from their modern features. But maybe I misunderstood. I wonder how this is handle for Chrome which must face the same issue.\nI think -march\u003dnative is not the answer as it applies whatever flags are supported by the CPU which does the compiling. That risks causing similar issues if the CPUs of the build machines support more modern features than the CPUs of our users.",
      "parentUuid": "fe9987d9_d1409854",
      "revId": "190a497eabf268377d0e18d2a7ffe5b876b7786c",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
